.. raw:: html

    <div class="sphx-glr-thumbcontainer" tooltip="Plots the first few 1D particle-in-a-box wavefunctions (~chemistrykit.quantum.systems.particle_in_box.ParticleInBox1D), each offset by its own energy level, then checks the textbook 3D cubic-box degeneracy pattern (~chemistrykit.quantum.systems.particle_in_box.ParticleInBox3D). These are the simplest exact solutions of Schrodinger&#x27;s 1926 time-independent equation -\frac{\hbar^2}{2m}\nabla^2\psi=E\psi inside hard walls: quantized energies E_n=n^2h^2/8mL^2 appear from the boundary conditions alone, with no quantization postulate.">

.. only:: html

  .. image:: /api/gallery/quantum/particle_in_box/images/thumb/sphx_glr_plot_01_particle_in_box_thumb.png
    :alt:

  :doc:`/api/gallery/quantum/particle_in_box/plot_01_particle_in_box`

.. raw:: html

      <div class="sphx-glr-thumbnail-title">Solving Schrodinger's equation for a particle in a box</div>
    </div>


.. only:: not html

 * :doc:`/api/gallery/quantum/particle_in_box/plot_01_particle_in_box`

.. raw:: html

    <div class="sphx-glr-thumbcontainer" tooltip="Hans Kuhn (1949) treated the pi electrons of a linear conjugated dye as free electrons in a one-dimensional box as long as the conjugated chain. With N pi electrons filling the box levels two at a time, the lowest absorption is the HOMO to LUMO jump, \Delta E=\frac{h^2}{8mL^2}(N+1), so a longer chain (more electrons and a longer box) absorbs at longer wavelength. conjugated_dye_absorption_wavelength implements this on top of ParticleInBox1D.">

.. only:: html

  .. image:: /api/gallery/quantum/particle_in_box/images/thumb/sphx_glr_plot_02_kuhn_free_electron_dye_thumb.png
    :alt:

  :doc:`/api/gallery/quantum/particle_in_box/plot_02_kuhn_free_electron_dye`

.. raw:: html

      <div class="sphx-glr-thumbnail-title">Kuhn's free-electron model of conjugated dye color</div>
    </div>


.. only:: not html

 * :doc:`/api/gallery/quantum/particle_in_box/plot_02_kuhn_free_electron_dye`

.. raw:: html

    <div class="sphx-glr-thumbcontainer" tooltip="Predicts HCl&#x27;s fundamental IR absorption from a harmonic oscillator, compares it against the exact (anharmonic) Morse-potential fundamental and first overtone, then inverts the observed 0\to1/0\to2 band positions to recover the spectroscopic constants \omega_e/\omega_ex_e -- exactly the way real anharmonicity constants are measured from an observed overtone spectrum.">

